## Why This Is Asked
Tests substring DP and reconstruction in a practical string-cleanup task, common in data processing pipelines at scale.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings: dp[i][j] captures minimal deletions for s[i..j]
- Reconstruction: recover exact deletions and resulting palindrome
- Complexity: O(n^2) time and O(n^2) space; can be optimized with path tracking

## Code Example
```javascript
function minDeletionsPalindrome(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j]) dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j-1];
      else dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]) + 1;
    }
  }
  return {deletions: dp[0][n-1], dp};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reconstruct the actual palindrome after deletions?
- How to optimize to O(n) space while keeping reconstruction capability?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical DP over partitions with a non-trivial subproblem (distinct counts) and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over contiguous partitions
- Precomputation of distinct counts for all subarrays
- Backpointers for block reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
// Distinct counts precomputation
for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
  const freq = new Map();
  let dist = 0;
  for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
    const x = logs[r];
    if ((freq.get(x) || 0) === 0) dist++;
    freq.set(x, (freq.get(x) || 0) + 1);
    distinctCount[l][r] = dist;
  }
}

// DP
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = distinctCount[0][i-1];
for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
  for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
      const val = dp[j][t-1] + distinctCount[j][i-1];
      if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space if n is large (e.g., using rolling arrays)?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks instead of exactly k, and still reconstruct the blocks?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests clean 0/1 DP layering with an additional teleport operation, requiring careful handling to avoid double-counting and efficient O(mn) time complexity with minimal extra memory overhead.

## Key Concepts
- 0/1 DP layering with an additional operation constraint
- Row/column prefix maxima for efficient teleport transition computation
- Path reconstruction under dual-state DP representation

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSum(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Initialize first cell
  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = grid[0][0];

  // Fill DP tables
  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;

      // Regular moves without teleport
      dp0[i][j] = Math.max(
        i > 0 ? dp0[i-1][j] : 0,
        j > 0 ? dp0[i][j-1] : 0
      ) + grid[i][j];

      // After teleport or using teleport now
      dp1[i][j] = Math.max(
        i > 0 ? dp1[i-1][j] : 0,
        j > 0 ? dp1[i][j-1] : 0,
        // Teleport from any cell in same row/column
        ...getTeleportOptions(dp0, i, j)
      ) + grid[i][j];
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on ordered subsequences with reconstruction, a common interview pattern that scales gracefully from beginner to moderate.

## Key Concepts
- State dp[i][t] represents best sum ending at i as the t-th pick
- Transition uses max over previous indices; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space/time trade-offs; potential O(nk) optimization via running maxima

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDotSubsequence(A,B,k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][1] = A[i]*B[i]; par[i][1] = -1; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best=-Infinity; let arg=-1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1] > best){ best=dp[j][t-1]; arg=j; }
      }
      if(best>-Infinity){
        dp[i][t] = best + A[i]*B[i];
        par[i][t] = arg;
      }
    }
  }
  let bestEnd=-1, bestVal=-Infinity;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[i][k] > bestVal){ bestVal=dp[i][k]; bestEnd=i; }
  const indices=[]; let i=bestEnd, t=k;
  while(i>=0 && t>=1){ indices.push(i); i=par[i][t]; t--; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {value: bestVal, indices};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space using prefix maxima per t-1?
- How would you extend to handle k up to n with space-saving tricks?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem introduces a dynamic programming angle where subarrays are weighted by their block index. It tests the ability to transform interval sums into prefix sums, derive a two-dimensional DP over position and block count, and implement solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) interval sum computation
- 2D DP over position and block count
- Solution reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k); Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScorePartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = [0];
  for (let x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1] + x);
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = P[i];
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
        const score = dp[p][t-1] + (P[i] - P[p]) * t;
        if (score > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = score;
          parent[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct partitions
  const partitions = [];
  let end = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = parent[end][t];
    partitions.unshift([start+1, end]);
    end = start;
  }

  return { maxScore: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests DP partitioning with a non-trivial per-segment cost that depends on both sum and min, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact k segments
- Precompute minVal(l,r) in O(n^2)
- Transition uses prefix sums and minVal; reconstruct with parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxCost(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  // minVal[l][r]
  const minVal = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for(let l=1;l<=n;l++){
    let m = A[l-1];
    minVal[l][l] = m;
    for(let r=l;r<=n;r++){
      m = (r===l) ? A[l-1] : Math.min(m, A[r-1]);
      minVal[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = (pref[i]-pref[0]) * minVal[1][i]; par[i][1] = 0; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p]) * minVal[p+1][i];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

